      en: "The Garango Royal Site in southeastern Burkina Faso was the political and ritual center of the Gurunsi Kingdom, ruled by the Moré dynasty in the 19th century. The complex includes traditional mud-brick palaces, royal tombs, and ceremonial grounds still used by local communities. Archaeological excavations have revealed continuous habitation since the 15th century. The architecture exemplifies Sudano-Sahelian style with thick pisé walls, flat roofs, and decorated facades. Located near the village of Garango, about 30 km northeast of Tenkodogo, the site was submitted to UNESCO's Tentative World Heritage List in 2019 as a cultural landscape.",
